    "PROOF": {
      "L0_DEGREE": "Cell domain as in the T270/T269 results: 22 triple labels + 27 pair labels cover 1..49, so every number has frequency 2 or 3; overlap <= 1 makes the numbers of a ticket reach disjoint neighbour sets, so deg(v) = 6 + t(v). Profile 5^12 1^6 0^2 gives degrees 11^12, 7^6, 6^2, |E| = 93. T counts all triangles of the overlap graph G. The argument uses only G, so it excludes every simple graph with these degrees, whether or not tickets realise it.",
      "REMARK_NOT_A_CELL_CLAIM": "S2 alone gives O >= 2c >= 28 for c >= 14, so the same lemmas show every graph with these degrees has T = 270 or T <= 262. This task reports cell statuses only for T = 263..268.",
      "S10_RESULT": "For T in 263..268 the only admissible c is 0 (S2-S3, S9), and c = 0 forces T = 270 (S4). Hence no graph with degrees 11^12 7^6 6^2 has T in {263,...,268}: all six cells are empty.",
      "S1_WEDGES": "W = 12*55 + 6*21 + 2*15 = 816. A wedge is closed iff it lies in a triangle, each triangle closes exactly 3 wedges, so O = W - 3T. For T = 263..268, O = 27, 24, 21, 18, 15, 12; in every cell O <= 27.",
      "S2_ARM": "For an edge uv, the open wedges with arm uv number (deg u - 1 - k) + (deg v - 1 - k), k = |N(u) & N(v)|; each open wedge has two arms, so these terms (all >= 0) sum to 2O. H = the 12 degree-11 vertices, L = the other 8, c = e(H, L). For u in H, x in L: k <= deg x - 1, so the term is >= 11 - deg x >= 4. Hence 4c <= 2O <= 54 and c <= floor(54/4) = 13 (per cell: c <= floor(O/2) = 13, 12, 10, 9, 7, 6).",
      "S3_PARITY": "sum_{u in H} deg u = 132 = 2 e(H) + c and sum_{x in L} deg x = 54 = 2 e(L) + c, so c is even: c in {0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12}.",
      "S4_C0": "c = 0: e(H) = 66 = C(12,2), so G[H] = K12; e(L) = 27 = C(8,2) - 1, and the degrees 7^6 6^2 force the one missing L pair to join the two degree-6 vertices. G = K12 + (K8 - e), T = 220 + (56 - 6) = 270, not in 263..268.",
      "S5_F": "c > 0: m = c/2 = 66 - e(H) pairs of H are non-adjacent; F = graph of these pairs on H, 1 <= m <= 6. For u in H, 11 = deg u = (11 - d_F(u)) + deg_L(u), so deg_L(u) = d_F(u): the H vertices with an L-neighbour are exactly S = non-isolated vertices of F, s = |S| <= 2m. d_F(u) <= m. For an F-edge xz the F-edges at x or z are d_F(x) + d_F(z) - 1 distinct edges, so d_F(x) + d_F(z) <= m + 1.",
      "S6_LEMMA_A": "Class H:HH (centre in H, both ends in H). For an F-edge xz: of the 10 vertices of H - {x,z}, x is non-adjacent to the d_F(x) - 1 vertices of N_F(x) - {z}, z to d_F(z) - 1, so at least 12 - d_F(x) - d_F(z) >= 11 - m >= 5 are common neighbours w, each giving the open wedge x-w-z (x, z non-adjacent). The wedge determines its end pair {x,z}, so different F-edges give different wedges: class H:HH >= m(11 - m).",
      "S7_LEMMA_B": "Class H:HL (centre in H, one end in H, one in L). For an H-L edge ux: N_H(u) has 11 - d_F(u) vertices; those adjacent to x lie in N(x) - {u}, at most deg x - 1 <= 6, so at least max(0, 5 - d_F(u)) >= 5 - m vertices w give the open wedge w-u-x. Such a wedge has exactly one H-L arm (u-x), so different H-L edges give different wedges, and it has an end in L, so it is not in class H:HH. For m <= 5: class H:HL >= c(5 - m).",
      "S8_LEMMA_C": "Support refinement of S7: N(x) & H lies in S (S5) and N_H(u) misses u and N_F(u), so the w in N_H(u) adjacent to x number at most min(deg x - 1, s - 1 - d_F(u)). For c = 2, F = {ab}, S = {a, b}, s - 1 - d_F = 0: each of the two H-L edges gives all 10 w in H - {a,b}.",
      "S9_CASES": "c=2: H:HH = 10 (m(11-m) = 10) + H:HL >= 2*10 -> O >= 30. c=4: 2*9 = 18 + 4*(5-2) = 12 -> 30. c=6: 3*8 = 24 + 6*(5-3) = 12 -> 36. c=8: 4*7 = 28. c=10: 5*6 = 30. c=12: 6*5 = 30. Classes H:HH and H:HL are disjoint, so in every case O >= 28 > 27 >= O, a contradiction."
    },
